# PEPSE — Procedural Environment Platformer & Simulator Ecosystem

![Java](https://img.shields.io/badge/Language-Java_11%2B-orange?style=for-the-badge&logo=openjdk)
![Architecture](https://img.shields.io/badge/Architecture-Object--Oriented_Design-blue?style=for-the-badge)
![Engine](https://img.shields.io/badge/Engine-DanoGL-green?style=for-the-badge)
![Generation](https://img.shields.io/badge/Terrain-Procedural_%26_Infinite-purple?style=for-the-badge)

> A modular, 2D procedural open-world game built in **Java** leveraging advanced **Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)** patterns, procedural generation (Perlin-style noise), event-driven interaction systems, and realistic physics using the **DanoGL** engine framework.
---

## 🌟 Key Features

* **🎲 Seed-Based Procedural Generation:**
  * **Dynamic Terrain:** The world contour and height map are calculated dynamically on startup using seed-based noise functions (`NoiseGenerator`), ensuring a unique topography every session.
  * **Procedural Vegetation:** Trees and leaves are generated non-deterministically across valid terrain blocks with varying densities and heights.

* **⚡ Interactive Mechanics & Energy System:**
  * **Custom Action-State Machine:** Smooth animation state transitions (`IDLE`, `RUNNING`, `JUMPING`) with directional flipping and velocity handling.
  * **Resource Management:** Real-time energy constraints (Jumping: `-10%`, Running: `-0.5%`/step, Resting: `+1%`/step).
  * **Exhaustion State Physics:** If energy drops to zero, player inputs are locked, but realistic environmental physics (gravity, collision forces) continue to act on the character.
  * **Consumable Collectibles:** Tree-spawned fruits replenish player energy upon collision (`+10%` per fruit) and trigger dynamic lifecycle respawns.

* **🍃 Event-Driven Environmental Interactions:**
  * **Observer / Callback Pattern:** Jumping triggers a visual shift in the ecosystem — surrounding tree leaves rotate dynamically and fruits undergo color variations upon jump events.
  * **Wind & Micro-Animations:** Background leaves feature continuous subtle transition dynamics to simulate wind.

---
pepse/
├── PepseGameManager.java     # Game entry point, loop initialization, and camera tracking
├── world/
│   ├── Avatar.java           # Player controller, energy management & state machine
│   ├── Block.java            # Immutable base building block for physics boundaries
│   ├── Terrain.java          # Procedural ground generator (Noise & Block calculation)
│   ├── Sky.java              # Background sky environment renderer
│   ├── Night.java            # Day/Night atmospheric overlay
│   └── Sun.java / SunHalo    # Celestial movement & lighting effects
└── trees/
├── Flora.java            # High-level environmental facade & plant manager
├── Tree.java             # Structural composite/facade grouping trunks, leaves, and fruits
├── Leaf.java             # Animated leaf object with collision & movement behavior
└── Fruit.java            # Interactive energy-replenishing game object

---

## 📐 Object-Oriented Design & Architectural Insights

### 1. Multi-Tiered Facade Pattern (`Flora` & `Tree`)
To streamline ecosystem management, two levels of abstraction were used to decouple individual game elements from the main game loop:
* **`Tree` Class (Local Facade / Composite):** Groups and manages individual trunk blocks, leaf arrays, and fruit elements. It treats a tree as a single entity during initialization and manipulation while providing clean aggregated access to the `GameManager` and `Flora` classes.
* **`Flora` Class (Global Facade):** Acts as the high-level manager for the entire vegetation subsystem, coordinating plant generation across generated terrain regions.

### 2. Class Specialization vs. Composition (`Leaf` & `Fruit`)
* **`Leaf` (Extends `Block`):** Inheriting from `Block` was chosen over composition because `Leaf` requires both physical spatial bounds and complex animation/movement states triggered by jump callbacks, justifying a dedicated subclass.
* **`Fruit` (Extends `GameObject`):** Inheriting directly from `GameObject` allowed fine-grained control over collision tags and energy interaction logic while decoupling it from static ground physics.

### 3. Future Architectural Trade-offs & Refactoring Reflections
* **Strategy Pattern Considerations:** During development, replacing hardcoded leaf and fruit animations/collisions with a **Strategy Pattern** was evaluated (using an interface with dedicated collision and movement strategy contracts). While inheritance was selected due to implementation timelines, transitioning to a Strategy Pattern in future iterations would further enhance modularity by decoupling physics components from rendering animations.

---

## 🔬 Core Implementation Highlights

### 1. Procedural Terrain Generation
Instead of pre-rendered tilemaps, terrain height at any continuous point $x$ is computed mathematically using noise offsets relative to the window dimensions:

```java
public float groundHeightAt(float x) {
    float noise = (float) noiseGenerator.noise(x, NOISE_FACTOR);
    return groundHeightAtX0 + noise;
}

### 2. Event-Driven Jump Callbacks (Observer Pattern)

The `Avatar` delegates actions to the environment using a decoupled `Runnable` callback mechanism without hard-coding direct dependencies on tree elements:

```java
// Avatar.java
if (inputListener.isKeyPressed(KeyEvent.VK_SPACE) && getVelocity().y() == 0) {
    if (this.energy >= JUMP_ENERGY) {
        this.energy -= JUMP_ENERGY;
        this.transform().setVelocityY(JUMP_LAUNCH_VELOCITY);
        if (this.jumpEffect != null) {
            this.jumpEffect.run(); // Triggers foliage movement & fruit transformations
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 🚀 Getting Started

### Prerequisites
* **Java Development Kit (JDK):** Version 11 or higher.
* **DanoGL Engine:** `DanoGL.jar` library added to the project classpath.

### Compilation & Execution

1. **Clone the repository:**
   ```
   git clone https://github.com/your-username/pepse-procedural-world.git
   cd pepse-procedural-world
   ```
2. Compile the source code:
javac -cp "lib/DanoGL.jar:." src/pepse/PepseGameManager.java -d bin/

3. Run the game:
java -cp "lib/DanoGL.jar:bin" pepse.PepseGameManager

---

## 🎮 Controls

| Action | Control Key | Energy Cost | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Move Left / Right** | `←` / `→` Arrow Keys | `-0.5%` / tick | Locks when energy = 0% |
| **Jump** | `Spacebar` | `-10%` per launch | Triggers leaf & fruit reaction |
| **Rest / Idle** | *No Input* | `+1.0%` / tick | Replenishes energy up to 100% |
| **Eat Fruit** | *Walk into Fruit* | `+10.0%` gain | Instantly consumed & scheduled to respawn |

---

## 🛠️ Tech Stack & Concepts Demonstrated

* **Language:** Java 11+
* **Engine / Library:** DanoGL (2D Game Development Framework)
* **Design Patterns:** Facade Pattern, Observer/Callback Pattern, State Pattern, Composite Pattern
* **Physics & Math:** Kinematic motion, collision handling, 1D/2D Noise generation, vector transformation
